Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 23:48:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?kn5000:=20remove=20the=20SNS=20payload-checksum?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20write=20tap=20=E2=80=94=20THE=20"Sound=20Name=20Error"=20cur?=
 =?UTF-8?q?e?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The KN5000 play screen now shows real voice names — RIGHT1 Piano, RIGHT2 Bigband
Brass, LEFT Modern E.P.1 — on both a fresh (factory-init) NVRAM and a persisted
one, with the SubCPU cooperative scheduler healthy (XSSP bounded ~0x40680 across
40s, no stack leak).

Root cause, found by building UPSTREAM mainline MAME's kn5000 as a third oracle:
upstream WORKS (real names) despite being a minimal skeleton with a raw inter-CPU
latch and none of this overlay's added HLE — because it has no SNS checksum tap.
Our overlay installed a write tap on the MainCPU's DRAM[0xFFD4] that intercepted
Boot_DisplayScreen's clearing of that word and substituted a payload checksum
computed from the DRAM checksum regions (0xF180/0xF980) at that early instant.
The firmware's SubCPU_Payload_Verify treats DRAM[0xFFD4]==0 as "no stored
checksum -> do a fresh decompress-and-transfer of the SubCPU firmware payload";
the tap's non-zero (and wrong-for-that-moment) value instead sent it down the
"payload already valid, skip the transfer" path, so the SubCPU ran with no/stale
firmware and never answered the MainCPU's 0x2B sound-name query -> "Sound Name
Error", and its scheduler idled with no ready task and leaked its stack (INTT3)
into the command handler. Removing the tap restores the fresh transfer.

Isolation: with ONLY the tap removed (this overlay's tmp94c241, latch wrappers and
audio HLE otherwise unchanged) all three names appear and XSSP stays bounded, so
the earlier upstream-tmp94c241 / raw-latch / HLE-stub experiments are NOT needed.
KN5000-only; -validate clean for the whole binary; KN7000 home unaffected
(Concert Grand / Bigband Brass / Modern E.P.). Payload transfer path untouched
(e6b4cf7 preserved). See side-quests/findings/kn5000_driver_findings.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mame/matsushita/kn5000.cpp | 47 ++++++++++++----------------------
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 30 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mame/matsushita/kn5000.cpp b/src/mame/matsushita/kn5000.cpp
index f37ddec..99eb934 100644
--- a/src/mame/matsushita/kn5000.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/matsushita/kn5000.cpp
@@ -517,36 +517,23 @@ void kn5000_state::machine_start()
 	m_keybed_timer = timer_alloc(FUNC(kn5000_state::keybed_scan), this);
 	m_keybed_timer->adjust(attotime::from_msec(1), 0, attotime::from_msec(1));
 
-	// SNS NMI emulation: intercept Boot_DisplayScreen's clearing of DRAM[0xFFD4]
-	// and substitute the correct payload checksums computed from current DRAM state.
-	// On real hardware, the NMI handler (EF08D4) computes these at power-off; we
-	// compute them at boot time using the same one's-complement algorithm.
-	m_maincpu->space(AS_PROGRAM).install_write_tap(
-		0xFFD4, 0xFFD5,
-		"sns_payload_checksum_w",
-		[this](offs_t offset, u16 &data, u16 mem_mask)
-		{
-			// Only intercept the 16-bit zero-write from Boot_DisplayScreen
-			if ((mem_mask & 0xFFFF) == 0xFFFF && data == 0x0000)
-			{
-				address_space &space = m_maincpu->space(AS_PROGRAM);
-				// Region 1: DRAM[0xF180], 0x800 words — one's complement sum
-				uint32_t sum = 0;
-				for (int i = 0; i < 0x800; i++)
-					sum = (sum + space.read_word(0xF180 + i * 2)) & 0xFFFF;
-				const uint16_t cksum1 = ~sum & 0xFFFF;
-				// Region 2: DRAM[0xF980], 0x280 words
-				sum = 0;
-				for (int i = 0; i < 0x280; i++)
-					sum = (sum + space.read_word(0xF980 + i * 2)) & 0xFFFF;
-				const uint16_t cksum2 = ~sum & 0xFFFF;
-				data = cksum1;
-				space.write_word(0xFFD2, cksum2);
-				LOGMASKED(LOG_RESET, "SNS payload checksum: cksum1=0x%04X cksum2=0x%04X at PC=%06X\n",
-					cksum1, cksum2, m_maincpu->pc());
-			}
-		});
-
+	// NOTE: an earlier "SNS payload-checksum" write tap on DRAM[0xFFD4] was REMOVED
+	// here (2026-07-20) — it was the root cause of the KN5000 "Sound Name Error".
+	//
+	// Boot_DisplayScreen clears DRAM[0xFFD4] to 0 during boot.  The firmware's
+	// SubCPU_Payload_Verify treats a zero there as "no valid stored payload
+	// checksum" and therefore performs a FRESH decompress-and-transfer of the
+	// SubCPU firmware payload over the inter-CPU latch.  The old tap intercepted
+	// that clear and substituted a checksum computed from the DRAM checksum
+	// regions (0xF180/0xF980) as they stood at that early instant — before those
+	// regions hold the values the verify expects — so the firmware instead took
+	// the "payload already valid, skip the transfer" path.  The SubCPU then ran
+	// with no (or stale) firmware and never answered the MainCPU's 0x2B sound-name
+	// query: RIGHT1/RIGHT2/LEFT showed "Sound Name Error".  This also matches
+	// upstream mainline MAME, whose kn5000 has no such tap and boots to real voice
+	// names.  With the tap gone, the SubCPU receives its payload, its cooperative
+	// scheduler stays healthy (XSSP bounded, no stack leak), and it replies to the
+	// name query.  See side-quests/findings/kn5000_driver_findings.md.
 }
 
 void kn5000_state::machine_reset()
